payload/.agents/skills/joycraft-implement/SKILL.md:59:Implement the spec and run meaningful checks and any relevant rendered inspection during implementation. Do not defer ordinary checks to the batch boundary. Run tests in the tiers of [running tests](../../../.joycraft/references/testing.md#running-tests). Write one focused test per acceptance `Check:`, from the spec rather than the code, and tag it with its queue criterion ID as `[AC:<criterion-id>]` in the test name or a comment; if a criterion or test looks wrong, report a spec gap instead of editing it to pass. When the implementation is finished, set it `in-review` with `node .joycraft/runtime/completion.mjs submit --queue <queue> --spec <id>`. A passing test suite, commit, or empty ready set does not accept the work.
payload/.agents/skills/joycraft-verify/SKILL.md:51:Create a schema-v1 verification report tied to that subject, using the shared fields in [.joycraft/contracts/types.d.ts](../../../.joycraft/contracts/types.d.ts). For every declared criterion, provide PASS observations for its required evidence kinds. Keep unfiltered command output and its real exit status in hashed, project-relative receipts. A source scan supports only structural observations. Visual observations require a reviewer-inspected render plus route, state, viewport, interactions, and outcomes. Take runtime evidence once from the spec's own tests (the packet's `commands.focused`), never the project gate ([running tests](../../../.joycraft/references/testing.md#running-tests)); other evidence with the package's recorded tool, start command and evidence command from the packet's `reviewLoop` (`section`: the `Review loop` section of `AGENTS.md`; `local`: this machine's host tools), within that tool's `canShow`. A claim beyond `canShow`, or in a package the user skipped, is an agreed limit, not `UNVERIFIED`: check what the agreed tool can show, say in plain words how you checked, and leave what is left to the parent agent. A picture-only tool gives `interactions: []`. `UNVERIFIED` means a check that should have run and did not: the tool broke, the app did not start, or a render was saved but not inspected. On a tool break, first repair with what the user agreed to: restart the dev server, free the port, relaunch the browser or simulator, retry once. A repair never installs, upgrades or reconfigures anything. If it still fails, record `UNVERIFIED` with `cause: 'tool-break'` and the reason. Mark a failing result or an unresolved concern as `FAIL` or `UNVERIFIED` truthfully.
